body:
I'm trying to send email using Google SMTP server but its throwing exception:
```
An unhandled exception occurred while processing the request.
Win32Exception: GSSAPI operation failed with error - An invalid status code was supplied (Unknown error).

System.Net.Security.NegotiateStreamPal.AcquireCredentialsHandle(string package, bool isServer, NetworkCredential credential)
SmtpException: Failure sending mail.

System.Runtime.ExceptionServices.ExceptionDispatchInfo.Throw()
```
At `Startup` Class:
```c#
 services.AddScoped<SmtpClient>((provider) =>
            {
                var config = provider.GetRequiredService<IConfiguration>();
                return new SmtpClient()
                {
                    Host = config.GetValue<String>("MailService:Smtp"),
                    Port = config.GetValue<int>("MailService:Port"),
                    Credentials = new NetworkCredential(
                            config.GetValue<String>("MailService:Username"),
                            config.GetValue<String>("MailService:Password")
                        )
                };
            });
```
And in `Controller`:
```c#
        private readonly SmtpClient _smtpClient;

        public ValuesController(
            SmtpClient smtpClient
        )
        {
            _smtpClient = smtpClient;
        }

        [HttpGet]
        public async Task<IActionResult> Get()
        {
            await _smtpClient.SendMailAsync(new MailMessage(
                from: "sender@domain.com",
                to: "receiver@domain.com",
                subject: "Test message subject",
                body: "Test message body"
                        ));
            return Ok();
        }
```

OS: Windows
.NET Version: .NET Core 2.1.1

I have this app:

```csharp
using System;

namespace ControlC
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Hello World!");
            Console.TreatControlCAsInput = true;
        }
    }
}
```

It always throws:

```console
Exception has occurred: CLR/System.IO.IOException
An unhandled exception of type 'System.IO.IOException' occurred in System.Console.dll: 'The handle is invalid'
   at System.ConsolePal.set_TreatControlCAsInput(Boolean value)
   at System.Console.set_TreatControlCAsInput(Boolean value)
   at ControlC.Program.Main(String[] args) in C:\Users\rich\ControlC\Program.cs:line 10
```

I wasn't expecting this. What is the correct behavior?

I am having some trouble getting the behavior I want with CTRL-C. I was using this property in a larger app and see the same behavior.  This is the minimal use case. I want to run code in the `CancelKeyPress` w/o it exiting before execution of the handler is complete.
